<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  v4043 — *** THIS FILE IS THE ENTIRE REASON THE APK EXISTS RATHER THAN THE PWA. ***

  The tree already ships manifest.webmanifest (display:fullscreen), and Chrome's "Install app" turns it into a
  chrome-less home-screen launcher — most of what this app is. It cannot be used on the LAN: Chrome only offers
  PWA install from a SECURE origin, and the SweK server is plain http://192.168.50.57:8787, so the prompt never
  appears there. A WebView has no secure-origin rule; it loads what this file permits. The PWA remains the right
  answer THROUGH THE TUNNEL (https), this is the right answer on the LAN, and neither replaces the other.

  *** THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS FILE WAS WRONG IN THE WAY THAT DOES NOT ANNOUNCE ITSELF, AND IS WORTH RECORDING. ***
  It listed <domain>192.168.0.0</domain>, <domain>10.0.0.0</domain> etc. under a narrow domain-config, with a
  confident comment about granting cleartext "to private ranges only". ANDROID'S <domain> MATCHES LITERAL
  HOSTNAMES, NOT CIDR — there is no subnet or range support at any Android version (confirmed against Android's
  own Network Security Config documentation, not from memory). "192.168.0.0" matches the host literally spelled
  192.168.0.0 and nothing else, so that config would NOT have matched 192.168.50.57: the app would have shipped
  looking carefully secured and failing to load the one address it exists to load, with a cleartext-blocked error
  that reads like the server being down.

  SO THE PLATFORM FLAG IS THE BLUNT ONE, BECAUSE THE NARROW ONE DOES NOT EXIST. Cleartext is permitted
  app-wide below. What keeps that honest is NOT this file but MainActivity's own routing: the WebView loads only
  the configured server and private-LAN hosts (isOwnServer), and hands every other URL to a real browser via
  shouldOverrideUrlLoading — so the app never navigates itself to an arbitrary public http:// origin. That is a
  behavioural control rather than a declarative one, which is weaker, and saying so is the point of this comment
  rather than leaving a reader to infer a guarantee the platform is not making.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
